# The Eighth Majlis

Sayyid Abbas says: although my honoured mother had argued so much with Phuphi-sahib, the very next day, mindful of her displeasure, she set Ghulam Imam and me to study. A certain Maulvi Sahib, a native of the east (*Purab*, the eastern provinces), had come to Delhi to study *fiqh* (Islamic law) and *hadith* (the traditions of the Prophet); his attainments in Arabic were very good indeed, but his skill in Persian prose composition had also become famous in the city. Amma-jan first placed us in his charge and fixed for him five rupees a month and one meal a day; he received five rupees a month and one meal a day from another house as well; and he also taught one or two boys without pay. In short, four or five of us boys studied with him. Ghulam Imam and I studied with him for about a year. In that time we read the two or three treatises on Arabic grammar and syntax (*sarf-o-nahw*) that remained; I looked at a book or so in Persian; and I wrote some compositions (*musawwada*). But the eagerness and ability that had grown day by day under Amma-jan's teaching were not there at all here, because, first, I had no classmate whose rivalry might spur me to work hard at my reading; all the boys read their own lessons separately; and Ghulam Imam, who was my partner, fell ill for a few days in between, and when he came back his lesson was far behind mine, so Maulvi Sahib began teaching him from where he was. Second, it was not his custom to hold examinations, and this made us still more carefree. Besides this, you know how ruinous the company of boys of one's own age can be; as long as we were there we talked by signs, and when we got our holiday we went off to the Turkman Gate to watch the kite-flying. In the end, little by little, we too took a liking to flying kites; the anna or two that Amma-jan gave me for buying things began to be spent on that as well; and in a few days everything I had read and written was forgotten [1]. Maulvi Sahib too saw that either the boy had once been in such a state that he would bring his preparation (*mutala'a*, the reading-ahead of the next lesson) well done, would learn his lesson every day, and when now and then he was asked something from earlier lessons would give a good answer too; or now he was getting worse day by day. Two or four times he admonished me, but when he saw that it had no effect on me, one day in my absence he came to the *deorhi* (entrance hall) and sent word of the whole matter to Amma-jan by the maid; he had also got some news of the kite-flying, and he told the maid this as well. Amma-jan told the maid to go, give Maulvi Sahib her greetings, and say: "You know that he is no longer of an age to be beaten; by God's grace he will soon enter his eleventh year; in two or three years, God keep him, he will be a young man. Second, he has a great deal of pride; I am afraid even to scold him; if he were to answer back it would be hard, and if he went off somewhere far away it would be harder still; then life would become hard for me to bear. But I hope that whatever I say he will accept. O God! may the world go on, and may my Sayyid Abbas go on with it; until today he has never gone against my word; but the times are very delicate, and nowadays it takes children no time to turn away from their parents. Well, I shall make him understand, however it may be done; but the arrangement regarding the boys in whose company he has gone astray is in your hands." Hearing this, the Maulvi went away. A little later, when I came into the house, I found Amma-jan kinder than on other days; there was no question of displeasure; on the contrary, she began to make much of me at every word. I had heard a vague rumour that Maulvi Sahib had come that day with some complaint, but with all this even that worry went away. At night, when everyone had eaten, Amma-jan, as was her custom, sat and began to chat about this and that. Meanwhile Apa Khadija came too, and I was already sitting there. Amma-jan said to her: "Look here, miss! Every day you tell me that you are very good at guessing riddles; now, will you guess a riddle of ours?" She said: "Tell me; if I understand it, I will answer." Amma-jan said: "Now tell me this: man is God's creation, and so is the animal; which of the two is better?" Before Baji-jan could answer, I said: "Amma-jan! If you allow me, I will tell you." She said: "Very well, my dear, you tell me." I said: "Man is better." She said: "Why, my dear? What has man more than an animal? Has an animal no eyes to see with? Or no ears to hear with? Or no nose to smell with? Or no tongue to taste with? Or does it not know its enemy? Or is it not glad to see its own kind? In my view, whatever is in man is in the animal too." I said: "But where does an animal have understanding like a man's?" Amma-jan said: "An animal understands a thing in a way that even a man does not. I have seen it written in a book that some men were going along a river in a boat, when suddenly a storm came, and by its force the boat ran aground on a sandbank; now there was no way for them to get out from there. A servant of God came to the river; he had a dog with him; when he saw the plight of the people in the boat, he at once put a small stick in the dog's mouth and pointed toward the boat; the dog understood his sign, and jumping into the river, cutting through the water, came close to the boat; but ahead there was quicksand, so how could it go further? Now it gazed into the faces of the people in the boat, as though making them understand that if they could do anything, they should do it. At last they understood too; they tied a piece of wood to a rope and threw it toward the dog; it let go of the stick in its own mouth right there, darted forward and took that piece of wood in its mouth, and pulling it along reached the bank and gave it to its master. Now its master held one end of the rope, and the people in the boat tied the other fast in the boat, and with the support of the rope the men got out of there one by one. Tell me truly: just as that dog at once understood what was in its master's mind and what the boatmen intended, if you had been there, would you have understood?" I said: "No, madam! That is beyond our understanding. Very well, we grant that the animal too has understanding like a man's; but where in it is love and faithfulness like a man's?" Amma-jan said: "My son! The faithfulness of the dog is well known, hundreds of people must know of it; but let that go too; does even the partridge count for anything? My father used to tell a story he had heard from some Englishman, that a man had so tamed a partridge that it would fly off wherever it liked, and after five or six days would come back to him again. When the partridge's owner fell ill and his condition grew grave, the partridge gave up all food and water. When he died and they carried him off to bury him, the partridge kept flinging itself upon his bier again and again. When the people had buried him and come away, there was a cypress tree right there, and it began to live in it; when it was hungry it would fly from there to his house; his mother would put some grain and water before it; as soon as it had eaten its fill, it would come back to the same cypress tree. At last, after three or four months, it died there. Now what greater faithfulness than this can there be?" I said: "Amma-jan! Well, I grant this too; but there are thousands of other virtues in man which animals do not have. Where in them is patience and steadfastness like ours? Where in them is discernment like ours? We make all kinds of things with our own hands; show me the craftsmanship of any animal! Where we fear an enemy, what schemes we devise for our protection; how should the poor animals know such things? Besides this, whatever you teach us we can learn; what will an animal learn?" Amma-jan said: "This is wrong too; there is not one of these things that is not found in animals. Prick a porcupine's body with a stick and see: it will at once curl up into the shape of a ball, and if it was moving it will stop still; that well-known proverb, 'one silence defeats a hundred', we have seen in it. My son! what else is patience and steadfastness? Next, if you ask about discernment, look at the squirrel: when it sits in a tree and eats its fruit, it first nibbles and nibbles away the skin and then eats the pulp. In craftsmanship the wasp and the spider and the weaver-bird are a thousand degrees better than you; a comb like the wasp's, a nest like the weaver-bird's and a web like the spider's you could never make. What understanding God has given the spider: when some big fly gets caught in its web and it sees that the web will break with the fly's struggling, it cuts the threads of the web near the fly with its teeth, and the fly at once gets free. And as for devising its own protection, no one can do it as the wild duck does. I have heard that when they fly together and come down to feed in some field, they first circle that field a score of times; when they have looked well and seen that there is no danger there, they come down; even then they stand still and for a good while (*do do ghari*, 'two *ghari*s at a time'; a *ghari* is about twenty-four minutes) keep looking silently this way and that with their heads raised; when they hear no sound anywhere, the duck that is biggest among them gives them some kind of signal, and they all spread out over the field in good order; but one duck keeps constant watch, and stands on one foot, and does not go near the grain to feed; when it has finished its watch, it gives the duck that is next to it a hard peck; and if that one is not roused at the first peck, it tugs at its feathers and begins to cry out as though someone were making a complaint. So if a man keeps watch for himself, he will keep only as much as this. And as for what you said, that an animal does not take training as a man does, that is your inexperience speaking; in this the dog and the horse are in no way less than man; trained dogs and horses have done such things in thousands of places as hardly any cultivated man would do."

At last, when we could find no answer, I said: "Amma-jan! Then you tell us what the difference is between man and animal." Amma-jan said: "My son! The difference between man and animal is this: God Most High has given man such power that, if he wishes, by the strength of his reason he can advance and advance until he reaches who knows where, while the poor animal cannot move one step beyond the state it is in. Look: the spider's web and the weaver-bird's nest and the wasp's comb, wherever you look and whenever you look, you will find of one and the same pattern; between the nest the weaver-bird must have built in the ark of the Prophet Noah and the nests of today there is not a hair's difference. And man's accomplishment, thanks to reason, has reached such a point that if some savage were to see the Jama Masjid of Delhi and the Taj Ganj of Agra [TN: the Taj Mahal and the quarter built around it], he would surely think it a world of enchantment (*tilismat*). In the same way, when a buffalo or an elephant goes into a river, it will swim across by the strength of its chest; and man, by the strength of his reason, has made all kinds of bridges and ships and made river travel so easy that you may go wherever you wish with your eyes shut. Likewise, when a horse is hungry, it will go into the jungle and fill its belly with grass and straw; and man, from the inventiveness of his heart, has devised such dishes that if some hill-man were to taste the food of Delhi and Lucknow, he would be left licking his fingers. My son! There was a time when men covered their bodies with the leaves of trees and so passed the days of their lives, and now is a time when one marvellous cloth after another comes from *vilayat* (abroad, especially England). Formerly there was no light in the world besides the light of the moon and the sun; then came lamps and candles and lanterns and chandeliers and glass shades; now I hear that in Calcutta the clever men of London have built a works from which at night every house and every shop in the city lights up of itself, and it keeps every house lit all night, and however large a house may be, it reaches every corner of it alike [TN: gas lighting]. Go further and look at English matches: formerly, only when people had lit a fire somewhere with grass and straw could a lamp be lit; after that came the sulphur match, and with it the need for grass and straw went; now, with this match, even fire is not needed; as soon as you rub the match a little on the lid of the box, it bursts into flame. Formerly there was no protection anywhere from the hot wind (*lu*); people would pass the day sitting in the shade of some tree or in a mountain cave; gradually houses began to be built, and their roofs and walls kept out the wind somewhat; then people dug *tahkhanas* (underground rooms); now such screens of *khas* (fragrant grass roots, kept wet to cool the air) and wheel-driven fans have come out that wherever you put them the air of paradise begins to blow. Formerly in hot countries there was nothing to cool water; in every season people drank the water as it was; thinking and thinking, people discovered saltpetre, and by shaking it in saltpetre water began to be cooled; then they found a method of storing ice, and used winter's ice in the hot season and the rains; but its management was rather difficult; unless some raja or nawab, or many merchants together, made the arrangements [2] for it, the business could not go on; now a machine has come out that, once you buy it for a thousand or five hundred, you can freeze ice wherever you wish and whenever you wish and as much as you wish. Formerly, what difficulty there was in covering a road: horse, camel, cart, *bahli*, chariot could not go more than fifteen or twenty *kos* (a *kos* is about two miles) in a whole day; where a man had to make a journey of thousands of *kos*, there were hundreds of rupees to spend as well; walking and walking, a man would be utterly worn out [3]; besides, the roads were not safe; thieves, snatchers, robbers and *thags* (bands who strangled and robbed travellers) would rob the travellers; for twenty *kos* at a stretch not a drop of water was to be had. Now see how safe the roads have become; at every step there are wells, canals and tanks; of highway robbers there is no trace anywhere. Before, a horse post had been set up from Calcutta to Peshawar, and a man would cover thousands of *kos* in seven or eight days without fatigue; now it does not take even that long. I hear that in England a railway train has come out in which an army can travel eight hundred and forty miles in a day and a night. Further, if news is to be sent somewhere, it can reach thousands of *kos* in no time at all; in the wire that is strung along the roadside, God knows what magic [4] there is, that just as you and I are talking now, so by it people sitting thousands of *kos* apart can converse. Further, what one hears of Roorkee [TN: the engineering college and workshops at Roorkee] is quite beyond understanding; work which a hundred craftsmen cannot do in a whole day, thousands of such tasks are done there in a snap of the fingers. In short, as far as you consider, you will see thousands of such things invented by man; this, certainly, is not in the animal. Now the man who makes no use of his reason, and stays content in whatever state he is in, what difference remains between him and an animal? Rather, if you ask the truth, he is worse than an animal, for the animal has no reason at all to make use of and by its strength take a step forward; but a man who, despite having reason, is caught in a whirlpool and does not strike out with hands and feet to get out, who will be more worthless than he? That is why the elders have said that no one is at a greater loss than the person whose two days pass alike [TN: a well-known saying, often attributed to the Prophet]; that is, a man ought always to keep advancing in learning knowledge and skill; what is known today was not known yesterday, and what will be known tomorrow is not known today. *Wari* (my dear)! Far from going forward, you are actually going backward. Today in your absence Maulvi Sahib came to the *deorhi*; he was saying that Sayyid Abbas has forgotten everything he had read and written; when he sits down to his lesson he reads the text wrongly, and in his preparation he cannot make out a single letter. My son! If you have no love of reading and writing, has your sense of honour flown away too? Were you not ashamed that until today Maulvi Sahib has never spoken to me sharply [TN: literally "has never gone from *hun* to *tun*", i.e. never once addressed him rudely], that I have a good name among the boys; if Maulvi Sahib grows angry and raises his hand, or says something harsh, how my pride will be humbled? Did it not occur to you that among my elders no one until today has been unlettered, and if I do not learn my four letters, how shall I show my face to people? Did you not understand that my mother has been wearing herself out over me since I was four, and how all her labour will go to waste [5]? Did you not see that any day now my marriage is due, and if I remain unlettered, who in the family will give me his daughter?"

In short, these words of Amma-jan's left me in such a state that I sank into the ground for shame. At the time I could find nothing to say, but the next day, when the time came to go to Maulvi Sahib's, I folded my hands before Amma-jan and submitted: "If it is your wish that I should learn something, then put me in the government school; if I am negligent in my studies after that, give me whatever punishment you see fit." Amma-jan liked this; that very day she had me sent to the school. Now my routine became this: from morning until nine I read Arabic and Persian with Maulvi Sahib, and in that time I also wrote my compositions; at nine I came home and ate; at ten I went off to school. On Sundays Amma-jan had given me leave to go hunting from morning until ten, and from two until five I would go to the river and learn swimming from Islam Beg. Until the age of fifteen this remained my way. I acquired mathematics, natural science, geography, history and English; and in Persian I read the *Shahnama*, the *Sikandarnama*, the *A'in-i Akbari* and so on; and in Arabic one or two books each of *fiqh*, *hadith* and *tafsir* (Qur'anic commentary); and in literature the *Alf Laila* (the Thousand and One Nights), the *Nafhat al-Yaman* and the *Tarikh-i Timuri* and so on, with Maulvi Sahib. And I learned to write Persian prose from him too. In short, whatever I know is the earning of those five years, and if you ask the truth, it is all owed to Amma-jan.
